      {
        "id": "test_1",
        "type": "testing",
        "catalyst_id": "chem_NiOOH",
        "description": "OER activity of pure NiOOH films in Fe-free 0.1 M MOH electrolytes",
        "conditions_json": {
          "reaction_type": {
            "value": "OER",
            "unit": "electrochemical"
          },
          "electrolyte": {
            "value": "0.1 M MOH (M = Li, Na, K, TBA, TMA)",
            "unit": "Fe-free solution"
          },
          "pH": {
            "value": "13.0",
            "unit": "pH (H2O)"
          },
          "technique": {
            "value": "cyclic voltammetry",
            "unit": "method"
          }
        },
        "results_json": {
          "Tafel_slope": {
            "value": 60,
            "unit": "mV/decade"
          },
          "Ni_II_Ni_III_oxidation_potential_shift": {
            "value": "negligible (Li+, Na+, K+); moderate shift (TBA+, TMA+)",
            "unit": "vs standard potential"
          },
          "OER_activity": {
            "value": "nearly identical K+ vs TMA+",
            "unit": "comparative"
          },
          "potential_pH_dependence": {
            "value": -60,
            "unit": "mV/pH"
          }
        },
        "source_text": "No modification of the NiII to NiIII oxidation potential is observed for Li+, Na+, and K+, while a moderate shift towards greater potential is observed for TBA+ and TMA+. ... it is found to be nearly identical for pure NiOOH when using K+ and TMA+ (Figure 1c). The Tafel slope is also found to be identical and equal to circa 60 mV/decade"
      },
      {
        "id": "test_2",
        "type": "testing",
        "catalyst_id": "chem_Ni_Fe_OOH",
        "description": "OER activity of Fe-containing Ni(Fe)OOH films in 0.1 M MOH electrolytes",
        "conditions_json": {
          "reaction_type": {
            "value": "OER",
            "unit": "electrochemical"
          },
          "electrolyte": {
            "value": "0.1 M MOH (M = K, Na, TMA) + 1000 ppm Fe",
            "unit": "solution"
          },
          "pH": {
            "value": "13.0 (H2O); 13.3 (D2O)",
            "unit": "pH"
          },
          "technique": {
            "value": "cyclic voltammetry",
            "unit": "method"
          }
        },
        "results_json": {
          "Tafel_slope": {
            "value": "35 (alkaline cations); 55 (TMA+)",
            "unit": "mV/decade"
          },
          "Ni3+_Ni2+_redox_shift": {
            "value": 78,
            "unit": "mV (K+ to TMA+)"
          },
          "potential_pH_dependence": {
            "value": -90,
            "unit": "mV/pH (pH > 12.5)"
          },
          "OER_activity": {
            "value": "decreases from alkaline cations to TMA+",
            "unit": "comparative"
          }
        },
        "source_text": "For Fe-containing films, the Ni3+/Ni2+ redox peak is largely influenced by the nature of the cation, and a shift towards greater potential as large as 78 mV was measured between K+ and TMA+. ... the OER activity for Ni(Fe)OOH films decreases between alkaline cations (K+ and Na+) and TMA+. Moreover, a change in the Tafel slope from 35 mV/decade to 55 mV/decade"
      },
      {
        "id": "test_3",
        "type": "testing",
        "catalyst_id": "chem_Ni_Fe_OOH",
        "description": "Isotopic effect on OER activity of Ni(Fe)OOH in H2O vs D2O",
        "conditions_json": {
          "reaction_type": {
            "value": "OER",
            "unit": "electrochemical"
          },
          "electrolyte": {
            "value": "0.1 M KOH/TMAOH",
            "unit": "solution"
          },
          "solvent": {
            "value": "H2O or D2O (99%)",
            "unit": "isotopic"
          },
          "pH": {
            "value": "13.0 (H2O); 13.3 (D2O)",
            "unit": "pH"
          }
        },
        "results_json": {
          "isotopic_effect": {
            "value": "significant activity decrease in D2O",
            "unit": "KOH solution"
          },
          "Tafel_slope": {
            "value": "increases in D2O",
            "unit": "mV/decade"
          }
        },
        "source_text": "for Fe-containing films, the use of deuterated water significantly decreases the OER activity in KOH solution. Not only the OER activity decreases, but also the Tafel slope increases when using deuterated water."
      }
    ],
